MOVES = [
("Move 1: underline the nouns. Every noun with its own state becomes a class.",
 "Reading the paragraph again: a <b>publisher</b> puts a <b>message</b> on a <b>topic</b>; a <b>subscription</b> "
 "reads it at its own <b>offset</b> and hands it to a <b>handler</b>; a <b>retry rule</b> decides what happens when "
 "the handler fails. A topic has a log, a next offset and a lock, all of which change: a class, and the one that "
 "matters. A subscription has a cursor, a thread and a state: a class. A message never changes once published, so it "
 "is a record &mdash; and it must be immutable for a real reason, not for tidiness: one object sits in the log and is "
 "handed to twenty handlers on twenty threads at once. A broker has the map of topics and the map of subscriptions: a "
 "class. A handler has no state at all, so it is an interface. A publisher is not a class here &mdash; it is whoever "
 "calls <code>publish</code>. And an offset is not a class either: it is a long, on the message and on the cursor.", 1),
("Move 2: for every verb, ask which class holds the state it touches. That class gets the method.",
 "\"Give a message its position\" touches the log and the next offset, so it belongs to the topic: "
 "<code>topic.append(...)</code>. \"Remember how far I have read\" touches one cursor, so it belongs to the "
 "subscription. \"Hand it over and survive a throw\" touches the cursor too &mdash; whether it moves is the whole "
 "question &mdash; so <code>deliver</code> is the subscription's as well. \"Find the topic, start a thread\" touches "
 "the two maps and the pool, so it is the broker's. Now notice the verb that is <i>not</i> in that list: \"send it to "
 "everybody\". There is no fan-out loop anywhere in this design and nothing is ever copied. Fan-out is three "
 "subscriptions reading one log at three different positions, which is why a fourth subscriber costs eight bytes and "
 "not another copy of every message.", 2),
("Move 3: every rule the interviewer can change mid-round goes behind an interface and is handed in.",
 "How to retry will change &mdash; twenty minutes in they will say \"a flat twenty milliseconds hammers a struggling "
 "downstream; back off exponentially, and never retry a message that can never work\". Where a hopeless message goes "
 "will change: a list today, a dead-letter topic tomorrow. Who is told will change: metrics, a lag alarm, a trace. "
 "What a subscriber sees will change: a filter. Each becomes a one-method interface that the subscription is "
 "<i>given</i> and never builds. This is where the patterns come from, not the other way round: a swappable rule "
 "behind an interface is <b>Strategy</b>; a rule that wraps another rule and adds to it is <b>Decorator</b>, and here "
 "the one that matters is <code>BoundedRetry</code>, which the broker wraps around every policy it is handed so that "
 "a rule written next year cannot retry a poison message until the end of time. A broker that announces \"a message "
 "landed\" without knowing what a metrics system is, is <b>Observer</b> &mdash; and in this system Observer is not a "
 "detail, it is the entire product. I do them; I do not announce them.", 3),
("Move 4: state that many callers change at the same time gets one owner and one lock.",
 "Two threads publish at the same instant. Assigning an offset and storing at it are two operations: both read "
 "<code>log.size()</code> as 7, both write at 7, and one message is simply gone &mdash; and worse, ArrayList's own "
 "internals corrupt under a concurrent add, so you may not even get a clean loss. So the two operations happen as "
 "one step, inside a lock owned by the class that owns the log: the topic. The second shared thing is less obvious "
 "and is the one that separates a good answer from a great one: the <i>waiting</i>. A dispatcher that asks \"is there "
 "anything at offset 7?\", sees nothing, and then goes to sleep can miss a publish that lands in the gap between the "
 "question and the sleep. So the check and the wait sit inside the same lock the publisher signals under, in a "
 "<code>while</code> loop, which also makes a spurious wakeup harmless. The lock is per <i>topic</i>, and no handler, "
 "no retry and no listener is ever inside it.", 4),
("Move 5: for each collection, ask what question is asked of it, and pick the shape that answers in O(1).",
 "\"What is at offset 91,204?\" is an ArrayList plus a base offset, so the index is a subtraction: O(1). \"How far "
 "behind is this subscriber?\" is one AtomicLong per subscription, and lag is tail minus cursor: O(1), never a scan "
 "of anything. \"Which subscription, which topic?\" are concurrent hash maps by id. \"What did we give up on?\" is an "
 "append-only list in the sink. But the shape worth arguing about is the one you do <i>not</i> pick: a queue per "
 "subscriber. It copies every message once per subscriber, it deletes on read, and with that single choice you have "
 "thrown away replay, late joiners and any lag number you could put on a dashboard. One log, N cursors. Cursor, not "
 "queue &mdash; every other decision in this design hangs off that one, including the fact that the stated price is "
 "honest: the log is bounded, so a subscriber can fall off the back of it.", 5),
("Move 6: anything with a life cycle is a state machine, and the order of operations is part of the design.",
 "One message's trip through one subscription: FETCHED under the topic's lock, DELIVERING once the lock is released, "
 "then ACKED if the handler returns, or RETRY WAIT and back to DELIVERING, or DEAD-LETTERED when the policy gives up. "
 "Both ACKED and DEAD-LETTERED lead to the same place &mdash; <code>cursor + 1</code> &mdash; because giving up must "
 "never stall a subscription, which is the commonest bug in a hand-rolled bus. That gives the order: read at the "
 "cursor with the lock held only for the read; release it; run the handler, which may take two seconds and may throw; "
 "retry or park; and only then move the cursor. The commit is the last statement, and that is the delivery guarantee "
 "written down: a crash in the middle redelivers, so this is at-least-once and handlers must be idempotent. Move the "
 "increment above the handler call and you have at-most-once, which is the right choice for a metrics ping. One line, "
 "the whole difference &mdash; say which one you picked before you write the loop. The subscription has a life of its "
 "own too: ACTIVE to PAUSED and back, then STOPPED, with the cursor kept across all of it.", 6),
("Move 7: yes, the lock makes one topic's publishes happen one at a time. Ask for how long, and what is inside it.",
 "The question you will be asked, and should ask yourself: if every publish takes the topic's lock, is this a queue "
 "with extra steps? It is, for about four tenths of a microsecond. Inside the lock there is a size read, an object "
 "construction, one array store, the retention check and a signal to whoever is waiting &mdash; and one spike worth "
 "naming out loud: when the log is full, trimming the head of an ArrayList copies ten thousand references, about "
 "three microseconds, which is exactly why a ring buffer is the first rung of the ladder. Everything else is outside: "
 "the handler, fifty microseconds in process and up to two seconds over HTTP; the retry backoff, which can be a "
 "minute; the listeners, called after the unlock inside a try/catch. So when ten threads publish to one topic at the "
 "same instant, the tenth waits about three and a half microseconds and then returns &mdash; and it never waits for a "
 "subscriber at all, which is the property publishers actually care about.", 7),
("Move 8: say the arithmetic, then name the ladder.",
 "An order service publishing two thousand messages a second into one topic holds that topic's lock for eight hundred "
 "microseconds in every second: busy eight hundredths of one per cent. A forty-thousand-a-second burst is sixteen "
 "milliseconds a second, one and a half per cent. And the lock is per topic, so the number of topics is irrelevant. "
 "Memory is the other number to say: ten thousand retained messages at roughly two hundred bytes is two megabytes per "
 "topic, and twenty subscribers on it cost twenty longs, not twenty copies. Then the ladder, cheapest first: replace "
 "the ArrayList with a ring buffer, so the trim is O(1) and the three-microsecond spike disappears &mdash; only Topic "
 "changes; then partition the topic into N logs with N locks keyed by the message key, which keeps per-key order and "
 "gives up total order across the topic, exactly the trade Kafka makes; then, if you really are at millions a second, "
 "drop the lock for a pre-sized ring with a published sequence per slot claimed by compare-and-set. There is a second "
 "ladder for the reading side: one thread per subscription is fine to a few hundred, and past that a bounded pool "
 "with a serial executor per subscription &mdash; and Subscription does not change, because it was always a Runnable "
 "with a cursor. Say the arithmetic first: climbing either ladder without it is complexity nobody asked for.", 8),
("Move 9: list what can go wrong, and write the test for each before the interview is over.",
 "Two threads publishing at the same instant (fifty of them, and every offset must be distinct). A dispatcher "
 "sleeping through a publish (the lost wakeup: the check and the await under one lock). A handler that throws on one "
 "message (retry, park, and the cursor must still reach the end). A retry policy that never gives up (the wrapper "
 "stops it, and the message is parked rather than retried forever). Retention overtaking a slow subscriber (the "
 "cursor is fast-forwarded and the gap is counted, so eleven delivered plus thirty-nine missed is exactly fifty). One "
 "handler that takes two seconds (six publishes must still return in no measurable time, and the other subscriber "
 "must get all six). A metrics listener that throws (the publish must still return its offset). Each of these is a "
 "few lines in FailureTests.java, which runs forty-six checks; a design that cannot show its tests is a claim.", 9),
("Move 10: now, and only now, name the patterns. Each one is the result of a move.",
 "Observer is not a pattern this design uses, it is the thing this design <i>is</i>: <code>publish</code> names a "
 "topic and never a subscriber, and that came out of move 3, not out of a pattern book. Strategy is the same move: "
 "how to retry, where to park, what to filter and what time it is are rules that will change, so each is a "
 "one-method interface the subscription is handed. Decorator is that move's wrapper, <code>BoundedRetry</code>, which "
 "adds \"you will give up eventually\" to every policy instead of copying the check into each one. Iterator is move "
 "5, quietly: the cursor is an iterator over a shared log, which is the reason a fourth subscriber costs eight bytes. "
 "State is move 6, the delivery life cycle plus the subscription's own. Builder is move 3 again, and this is one of "
 "the few LLDs where it genuinely earns its place: <code>SubscribeOptions</code> has five knobs and every one is "
 "optional, which is exactly the case a five-argument constructor handles badly. Singleton earned nothing &mdash; the "
 "broker is handed to its callers, so a test builds a fresh one per case. Factory is \"not yet\": topics appear "
 "through one <code>computeIfAbsent</code>, and it earns the name the day a topic needs a configuration of its own. "
 "Command is \"never\" here: a message is data, not an instruction. A pattern without a move behind it is decoration.", 10),
("Move 11: run SOLID as a check on the moves, one line each.",
 "S: a class has one reason to change, which move 2 gave you &mdash; the topic changes when storage changes, the "
 "subscription when delivery changes, a retry policy when that rule changes, the broker when the wiring changes, and "
 "nobody does two of those. O: exponential backoff, never-retry-this, a dead-letter topic and a file-backed store are "
 "each a new class behind an existing interface, and Topic and Subscription were never reopened to add any of them. "
 "L: any policy stands in for any other, because the contract is only \"a negative answer means give up\" and nothing "
 "asks which one it got. I: five interfaces with one method each, so every fake in the tests is a lambda. D: the "
 "subscription is handed its policy, its sink, its filter and its clock and never writes <code>new</code> on any of "
 "them &mdash; which is exactly why a test can hand it a clock that reports a five-minute backoff schedule in no time "
 "at all, and a policy that refuses to ever give up.", 11),
("Move 12: every twist the interviewer adds is one of five moves. Say which before you type.",
 "A new rule (exponential backoff, never retry a malformed payload, a different sink) is a new class behind an "
 "existing interface plus one line at the subscribe call. Someone new who wants to know (metrics, a lag alarm, "
 "tracing) is one more listener; the log and the cursors do not move. A new step in a life (paused, draining, "
 "quarantined) is one more state and one more checked transition. A new invariant across readers &mdash; each message "
 "handled by exactly one worker instead of all of them &mdash; is consumer groups, and it needs no new broker code at "
 "all: it is the same log with one filter per group member, which is move 3 on top of move 5. State that must outlive "
 "the process is the log behind a <code>MessageStore</code> and the cursor behind an <code>OffsetStore</code>: the "
 "append becomes a segment write and the commit becomes an offset commit, and every class keeps its name, which is "
 "why \"explain Kafka\" and \"explain this file\" turn out to be the same explanation at two different sizes. Page 05 "
 "has the code for each.", 12),
]
DERIVATION_LEAD = ("Run these on any LLD (parking lot, elevator, BookMyShow) and the class diagram, the lock, the tests, "
 "the patterns, SOLID and the answer to every twist fall out in that order; nothing is chosen up front, and nothing is "
 "named before the move that produced it. On this problem one decision carries everything: one append-only log per topic "
 "with a cursor per subscriber, instead of a queue per subscriber. Get that in move 5 and replay, late joiners, lag and "
 "cheap fan-out are all free; get it wrong and no amount of later cleverness buys them back.")

