HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns: the broker owns its topics and its subscriptions, and a topic owns its log of '
 'messages. Plain arrow = references: a subscription points at a topic it does <i>not</i> own, because many '
 'subscriptions share one topic and stopping one must not close it. Dashed lavender = handed in. Dotted blue = '
 'notifies. <b>Where state lives:</b> the topic has the log, the base offset, the retention counter and the one lock, '
 'and nothing else in the system writes an offset; a subscription has exactly one long &mdash; its cursor &mdash; plus '
 'its thread, its state and its counters; the broker has the two maps and the thread pool and no lock at all; a retry '
 'policy and a filter have no state, which is why one instance can serve every subscription. Notice what is <i>not</i> '
 'here: no queue anywhere, and no list of subscribers on the topic. A topic does not know who is reading it; the '
 'cursors do all the work, which is the whole design in one sentence.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics &mdash; and '
 'when you get to <code>Subscription.run()</code>, read the last two statements of the loop slowly, because they are the '
 'delivery guarantee. Each copy button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every '
 'follow-up\'s reference code, with an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (forty-six checks '
 'proving the claims of move 9; <code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> '
 'prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (fan-out versus competing consumers, and at-most-once '
 'versus at-least-once, come first); then type in the order of Main.java: the two enums, the records (Message, Dead, '
 'Event), the Clock, the one-method interfaces (Handler, Filter, RetryPolicy, DeadLetterSink, BrokerListener), two '
 'retry policies and the BoundedRetry wrapper, the in-memory sink, then Topic with its lock and its append and '
 'awaitAt, then SubscribeOptions, then Subscription with its dispatch loop and the commit as the last statement, then '
 'the thin Broker, then a main with fifty publishing threads.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Twenty minutes in: \"back off exponentially instead of a flat 20 ms, and never retry a message that can never work.\"", "twist", 10,
 "Two changes, both new classes, and nothing inside Topic, Subscription or Broker moves. ExponentialBackoff returns "
 "base shifted left by the attempt number, capped, and gives up after n attempts &mdash; 10 ms, 20 ms, 40 ms, then "
 "stop. \"Never retry a message that can never work\" is a wrapper rather than a new policy: NoRetryFor holds any "
 "policy and answers &minus;1 the moment the failure is of a type you have declared permanent, so it composes with "
 "the backoff instead of replacing it. The call site is one line: "
 "<code>opts().retry(new NoRetryFor(new ExponentialBackoff(6, 10, 1000), PermanentFailure.class))</code>. The seam "
 "that made this cheap is that the failure is passed to the policy, so telling a timeout from a malformed payload "
 "never needed a signature change.",
 sect(src, "final class ExponentialBackoff", "final class BoundedRetry") + "\n" + X("never retry a message that can never work", "a dead-letter TOPIC")),
("Fifty threads publish to one topic at the same instant. Prove you cannot duplicate or lose an offset, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading the log's size and storing at it: two threads both read 7, both write at 7, one "
 "message disappears, and ArrayList's internals corrupt on top of that. append does both operations inside the "
 "topic's lock, so no other publisher can run in that gap. The proof is not a count but a set: fifty threads wait on "
 "one latch, each publishes forty messages, and each collects the offsets it was handed. The test then checks that "
 "the two thousand offsets are two thousand distinct values, that the log's tail is exactly 2000, that every offset "
 "from 0 to 1999 reads back non-null (no hole), and that each publisher's own offsets increased. A subscriber "
 "counting in the background must also have seen all two thousand.",
 T("        // 1. fifty threads publish into ONE topic", "        // 2. fan-out")),
("One lock per topic. Have you just serialised the whole broker?", "non-functional", 5,
 "No, and the answer is arithmetic. The locked part of a publish is a size read, an object construction, one array "
 "store, the retention check and a signal: about four tenths of a microsecond. An order service at two thousand "
 "messages a second holds the lock eight hundred microseconds in every second, eight hundredths of one per cent; a "
 "forty-thousand-a-second burst is one and a half per cent. The lock is per topic, so ten thousand topics never meet. "
 "There is one spike worth naming before they find it: when the log is full, trimming the head of an ArrayList copies "
 "ten thousand references, about three microseconds &mdash; which is exactly why rung one of the ladder is a ring "
 "buffer, where only Topic changes. Rung two is partitions: N logs and N locks keyed by the message key, keeping "
 "per-key order and giving up total order. Rung three, at millions a second, is a pre-sized ring with a sequence per "
 "slot claimed by compare-and-set, and no lock at all.",
 sect(src, "final class Topic", "// \"the knobs are about to multiply\"")),
("A handler throws. Where is the cursor afterwards, and what guarantee have you just implemented?", "functional", 10,
 "The cursor has not moved, because the increment is the last statement of the loop and it runs only when deliver "
 "returns true. deliver calls the handler with no lock held; on an exception it asks the policy for a backoff, waits "
 "and tries again; when the policy answers a negative number it parks the message in the dead-letter sink and returns "
 "true anyway, because giving up must advance the cursor or the subscription is stuck forever on one bad message. A "
 "shutdown in the middle returns false, so that message is delivered again next time. That is at-least-once, and it "
 "is one line: move the increment above the handler call and you have at-most-once, which is right for a metrics "
 "ping. The price of at-least-once is duplicates, so handlers must be idempotent &mdash; and when the business code "
 "is not, a decorator that remembers message ids gets you there.",
 sect(src, "    private boolean deliver(", "    void pause()") + "\n" + X("exactly-once for the handler", "back-pressure")),
("A subscriber was down for an hour. It comes back. What does it see?", "functional", 5,
 "Whatever it had not finished, and nothing twice. Its cursor is the offset it will read next, committed only after a "
 "handler returned, so a restart from that number replays nothing it acknowledged and skips nothing it did not. "
 "Subscribing with fromOffset does exactly that in one line; fromEarliest replays all the retained history instead, "
 "which is what a brand-new analytics consumer wants. The hard case is the honest one: if retention dropped messages "
 "under the cursor while it was down, the dispatcher fast-forwards to the earliest surviving offset and adds the gap "
 "to a missed counter, so the loss is a number on a dashboard rather than a silence. Nothing else in the system "
 "notices any of this, because a cursor is a private long.",
 T("        // 3. a subscriber restarts", "        // 4. the handler throws") + "\n"
 + T("        // 6. retention overtakes a slow subscriber", "        // 7. one slow subscriber")),
("One subscriber takes two seconds per message. What happens to the publisher, and to the other subscribers?", "non-functional", 5,
 "Nothing happens to either. The publisher's call ends when the message is in the log, which takes under a "
 "microsecond, and it never looks at a subscriber; the slow handler runs on its own dispatcher thread with no lock "
 "held, so the only thing that grows is that subscription's own lag. The test publishes six messages, asserts the "
 "publish loop took no measurable time, asserts the fast subscriber received all six, and asserts the slow one is "
 "still behind. The junior version of this design calls every handler inside publish, which makes one slow "
 "subscriber a global outage and one throwing subscriber a fan-out that stops halfway. The cost of the good version "
 "is real and worth saying: a stuck handler stalls its own subscription forever, so production wraps delivery in a "
 "timeout, and back-pressure or retention has to bound the memory.",
 T("        // 7. one slow subscriber", "        // 8. a handler that always throws")),
("Now make it competing consumers: a group of three workers, each message handled by exactly one of them, and per-key order kept.", "twist", 10,
 "It needs no new broker code at all, which is the sign that move 5 was right. A group member is a normal "
 "subscription with a filter that accepts only its own slice of the key space, so the three of them read the same log "
 "at three cursors and each handles one message in three. Messages with the same key hash to the same member, so "
 "per-key order survives; a message with no key falls back to its offset, which round-robins. The honest price is "
 "that each member walks the whole log and skips two thirds of it, which is fine at these volumes and is exactly what "
 "real Kafka fixes by giving each member its own partition log instead &mdash; that is the partitions extension, the "
 "same idea one level down, and the reason consumer groups rebalance partitions rather than keys.",
 X("consumer groups", "never retry a message that can never work") + "\n" + X("partitions", "persistence")),
("The dashboard asks every subscriber's lag a thousand times a second. Make it O(1).", "non-functional", 5,
 "It already is. Lag is the topic's tail offset minus the subscription's cursor, which is two volatile reads and a "
 "subtraction, and nothing is scanned, counted or re-read. That is a direct consequence of one log with a cursor per "
 "subscriber: with a queue per subscriber you would be asking for the size of a concurrent queue, which is at best "
 "approximate and at worst O(n). The broker's lagReport walks the subscription map once and does that subtraction "
 "per subscriber, so the whole dashboard is O(number of subscriptions). The alarm on top is a listener plus a filter "
 "over the report, and it needs no new state anywhere.",
 X("lag metrics and an alarm", "partitions")),
("Where do messages that can never be processed go, and how do I stop a burst from eating the heap?", "twist", 5,
 "Two different answers. A message nobody can handle is retried on the policy and then parked in a dead-letter sink, "
 "never dropped and never blocked on; in production the sink is another topic, so the repair tool is another "
 "subscriber and a replay is another publish. Memory is bounded by retention: each topic keeps a fixed number of "
 "messages and trims the head, counting what it dropped, so a subscriber that falls off the window is fast-forwarded "
 "visibly instead of stalling everybody. If dropping is not acceptable, back-pressure is a gate in front of publish "
 "rather than a change to the broker: wait while the slowest subscriber on that topic is beyond a high-water mark, "
 "and fail loudly after a budget instead of buffering forever.",
 X("a dead-letter TOPIC", "exactly-once for the handler") + "\n" + X("back-pressure", "lag metrics and an alarm")),
("Persist it: messages must survive a restart. And then there are two processes.", "twist", 5,
 "The log goes behind a MessageStore and the cursors behind an OffsetStore, and no other class changes &mdash; the "
 "topic's ArrayList becomes a cache of the tail of a segment file, and a restart replays the file in order so the "
 "offsets come out identical. A subscriber resumes with one line, because its cursor was always just a number. Two "
 "processes is where an in-process broker stops being the right tool and you should say so: the log moves out to "
 "Kafka or Pulsar or a table with a monotonic id, the topic's lock becomes the partition leader's, and the cursor "
 "becomes a committed offset in the broker's own store. Every class here keeps its name, which is why the mapping "
 "table is worth memorising: it is the same explanation at two different sizes.",
 X("persistence", "replay from a point in time") + "\n" + X("the Kafka mapping", "Runs every extension")),
("Where does time come from, and how do you test a five-minute backoff schedule without waiting five minutes?", "design", 3,
 "One injected interface does both jobs: Clock answers what time it is and also performs the wait, with a default "
 "method so the production one is still a lambda. Nothing else in the system reads the wall clock or calls "
 "Thread.sleep. A test hands in a clock that records every requested wait and returns at once, so the exponential "
 "schedule can be asserted exactly &mdash; 10, 20, 40 &mdash; and the whole suite runs in milliseconds. The same seam "
 "stamps message timestamps, which is what makes seek-by-timestamp testable.",
 sect(src, "interface Clock", "// \"a handler will fail") + "\n"
 "// in a test: the clock records what it was asked to wait for, and never actually waits\n"
 "static final class TestClock implements Clock {\n"
 "    volatile long now = 1_700_000_000_000L;\n"
 "    final List<Long> sleeps = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n"
 "    public long nowMs() { return now; }\n"
 "    @Override public void sleepMs(long ms) { sleeps.add(ms); now += ms; }\n"
 "}\n"
 "broker.setClock(clock);\n"
 "// ... a handler that throws three times ...\n"
 "check(clock.sleeps.equals(List.of(10L, 20L, 40L)), \"the waits before giving up were 10, 20 and 40 ms\");\n"),
("Which pattern is where, and why did each one earn its place?", "design", 8,
 "None was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Observer is not a pattern this design uses, it is what the "
 "design is: publish names a topic and never a subscriber. Strategy is move 3 &mdash; retry, sink, filter and clock "
 "are rules that will change, so each is a one-method interface handed in. Decorator is that move's wrapper, "
 "BoundedRetry, which the broker puts around every policy so no rule, present or future, can retry forever; NoRetryFor "
 "is the same trick applied by a caller. Iterator is move 5: the cursor is an iterator over a shared log, and it is "
 "the reason a fourth subscriber costs eight bytes instead of a copy of everything. State is move 6, twice over: the "
 "delivery life cycle and the subscription's own. Builder is move 3 again, and this is the rare LLD where it earns "
 "its place, because SubscribeOptions has five knobs and all five are optional. Singleton earned nothing: the broker "
 "is handed to its callers, so each test builds a fresh one.",
 "// Observer: the whole product. publish names a topic; it has never heard of a subscriber\n"
 "long publish(String topicName, String key, String payload) { ... }\n"
 "Subscription subscribe(String subId, String topicName, Handler h, SubscribeOptions o) { ... }\n\n"
 "// Strategy: a rule behind a one-method interface, handed in, never built by the broker\n"
 "interface RetryPolicy { long backoffMs(int attempt, Exception failure); }\n\n"
 "// Decorator: add the invariant to every policy, present and future, instead of trusting each one\n"
 "new BoundedRetry(o.retry == null ? defaultRetry : o.retry, MAX_ATTEMPTS, MAX_BACKOFF_MS)\n\n"
 "// Iterator: one log, N cursors. This line is the entire fan-out mechanism\n"
 "private final AtomicLong cursor;            // the next offset to read; written by one thread\n\n"
 "// State: the delivery life cycle, and the subscription's own\n"
 "enum SubState { ACTIVE, PAUSED, STOPPED }\n"
 "if (deliver(m)) cursor.incrementAndGet();   // ACKED and DEAD-LETTERED both commit; nothing else does\n\n"
 "// Builder: five optional knobs, which is exactly the case a long constructor handles badly\n"
 "SubscribeOptions.opts().fromEarliest().retry(new ExponentialBackoff(5, 10, 1000)).filter(m -> \"IN\".equals(m.key()))\n"),
("Which SOLID letter is where in this code, and where would a Factory earn its place?", "design", 5,
 "S: the topic stores and hands back by offset, the subscription drives one cursor and one handler, a policy answers "
 "\"again, and how long\", the broker wires &mdash; changing the retry rule cannot break the log. O: exponential "
 "backoff, never-retry-this, a dead-letter topic and a file store were each a new class behind an existing interface, "
 "and Topic and Subscription were never reopened. L: any policy substitutes for any other, because the contract is "
 "only that a negative answer means give up, and no caller asks which policy it got. I: five interfaces with one "
 "method each, so every fake in the tests is a lambda. D: the subscription is handed its policy, sink, filter and "
 "clock and never writes new on any of them, which is why a test can hand in a policy that refuses to give up. "
 "Factory: topics appear through a single computeIfAbsent, which is a registry already; it earns the name the day a "
 "topic needs a configuration of its own &mdash; its own retention, its own partition count &mdash; because that is "
 "when construction stops being one line.",
 "// S: four classes, four reasons to change\n"
 "final class Topic { /* the log and the offsets */ }\n"
 "final class Subscription { /* one cursor, one handler, one thread */ }\n"
 "interface RetryPolicy { long backoffMs(int attempt, Exception failure); }\n\n"
 "// D + I: handed in, one method each, so a test fake is a lambda\n"
 "cap.configure((attempt, failure) -> 0, parked);       // \"always retry, immediately\" -- and BoundedRetry stops it\n"
 "broker.setClock(() -> fixedInstant);\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. This one line is the registry; it earns the name when a topic needs a config\n"
 "Topic topic(String name) { return topics.computeIfAbsent(name, n -> new Topic(n, retention)); }\n"
 "// the day it becomes:  topics.computeIfAbsent(name, n -> topicFactory.create(n, configFor(n)));\n"),
]

